# Lesson 4, new exercise: the right play that doesn't work. The diamond finesse is the only chance, and East has the king: down one.
title: A good finesse that loses
id: L04-14
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.1
student: N
board: 68
dealer: W
vulnerable: both

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  A6     KQJT5  AQ4    J43
  E  QJT    764    KJT    KQT8
  S  K842   9832   752    A6
  W  9753   A      9863   9752

auction:
  W Pass
  N 1H     = 13+ points, at least four hearts
     not *: 17 points and five hearts: open your long suit at the one level.
  E Pass
  S 2H     = 6-9 points, 3+ hearts
  W Pass
  N 4H     = Game
     not *: You have 18 points counting your doubleton, and partner has at least 6: enough for game.
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1♥ and partner raised to 2♥. With 18 points counting
  distribution, you bid game. You are declarer, because you bid hearts
  first, and so East will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 4
  spades: 0 = Your ace and dummy's king.
  hearts: 1 = The ace.
  diamonds: 2 = The queen, unless West has the king and you lead toward your ace-queen, and your third diamond.
  clubs: 1 = Dummy's ace takes care of one club, and dummy can ruff the third. That leaves one.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♥. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: Which loser might go away? Which hand should lead that suit?
play:
  1   E CK  S CA  W C2  N C3
      note: Four losers, one too many. The only one that might go away is the ♦Q, if West has the king. You'll need to lead diamonds from dummy. Win in dummy.
  2   S H2  W HA  N H5  E H4
      note: You're in dummy. Start on trumps: you have to lose the ace anyway.
  3   W C9  N C4  E C8  S C6
  4   W C7  N CJ  E CQ  S H3
      note: Dummy ruffs, and you're in dummy, just where you want to be.
  5   S D2  W D3  N DQ*  E DK
      note: Lead a diamond toward your ace-queen.
      DQ key: West played low, so finesse the queen. It's your only chance to get rid of a loser.
      DQ not DA: With the ace, you're sure to lose two diamonds and go down. The finesse is your only chance: if West has the king, the queen wins.
  6   E SQ  S S2  W S3  N SA
      note: The finesse lost: East had the king. That happens about half the time.
  7   N HK  E H6  S H8  W S5
      note: Draw the rest of the trumps.
  8   N HQ  E H7  S H9  W S7
  9   N DA  E DT  S D5  W D6
      note: Cash the ♦A.
  10  N D4  E DJ  S D7  W D8
      note: Your last diamond is the fourth loser.
  11  E SJ  S SK  W S9  N S6
  12  S S8  W C5  N HT  E ST
  13  N HJ  E CT  S S4  W D9
      note: 9 tricks: 4♥ down one.

play recap:
  The plan: you counted four losers, one too many, and the ♦Q was the only
  one that might go away, if West held the king.

  You did everything right, and East had the king. A finesse loses about
  half the time, and nothing else would have worked on this deal. 4♥ was a
  good contract and you played it the right way: the cards just weren't
  where you needed them. Judge your play by the decisions you made, not by
  the result.
